{"kind":"request","text":"Stop the merge step from manufacturing staleness on every completed loop, using the succession signal that measurement supports. This replaces request f3ccfc1a, which I retired unimplemented after measuring it. That request implied succession should be read from the supersede act citing a same-path successor; measured against the real workroom, that signal is present for only 46 of 93 retired artifacts, because supersessions have not historically cited their successor, so its stated condition of satisfaction was unreachable. The structural rule is better: treat a retired artifact as succeeded when a later live artifact exists at the same path. That classifies 71 of 93 as succeeded and leaves 22 condemned, and the 22 look like genuine condemnations rather than misses. The dependency I had not seen: this rule is unsound while merge artifacts claim path dot, because 80 of 346 artifacts use that path and every later dot artifact would then rescue every earlier one, condemned ones included. Measurably, none of the 22 uncovered are at path dot, which is the symptom of that over-broad rescue rather than evidence of health. So the merge-artifact path repair f35c3447, whose chain is reported and stale, has to land first. Both together make the projection tell the truth about finished work; either alone does not.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced behind the merge-artifact path repair f35c3447, which must land first because the succession rule is unsound while artifacts claim path dot. Then: the fold treats a retired artifact as succeeded, rather than condemned, when a later live artifact exists at the same path, and stops propagating staleness through succession while retirement without a successor propagates exactly as today. Tests pin both directions and the boundary: a branch artifact superseded by its own merge artifact at the same path leaves the approval, the ready report and the commitment non-stale; an artifact retired with no successor at that path still makes them stale; and an artifact retired as wrong is not rescued by an unrelated later artifact appearing at the same path. Counts are reported before and after over the real workroom rather than asserted: today 93 retired artifacts, of which this rule classifies 71 as succeeded and 22 as condemned, and 62 of 89 satisfied-and-stale commitments are stale through this path. ProfileVersion advances; docs/concepts/staleness.md and docs/reference/gs/status.md state the distinction between an artifact replaced by its successor and one retired as wrong.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}